{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789410707,"nonce":"Xrt7lQey3FWs3auuZLTGC+0nR88=","title":"Cells: one core object per foreign object, read as the holder's own","message":"A language's compiled code reads its own objects at fixed offsets (Wren: obj_type at 0 and class at 16; Haxe: hl_type* at 0), on every send, so a raw address of the other language's object is unusable to it. The bridge interposes at calls, not loads. So an object held across languages needs a header of the holder's, and the question per allocation is whether it is that header or a bridge convention.\n\nBefore this issue a Wren object entering Haxe cost two allocations and two lookups per first crossing: a WrenRef (core object, HABSTRACT prefix, the shadow) and a face (an HL object of the emitted class allocated with hlp_alloc_obj, whose __ref field held the ref), plus a HaxeRef wrapper (16 B) on every Haxe→Wren crossing and a stand-in instance plus map entry per Haxe object in Wren.\n\nThe cell (caribou::cell) is the one core object standing for an object in the terms of the language holding it. Layout: [descriptor][object Value][front]. Word zero is a TypeDesc whose hl_type prefix the holder's adapter fills as its own header: Ash mirrors the class the program declares for the object's published type (a bitwise copy of the class's hl_type; hlp_get_obj_rt is built for the real type first so the mirror never builds it and never has its mark_bits written), so to HashLink the cell is an instance of that class: Std.isOfType, Type.getClass, casts, virtual dispatch and == all work through the shared class data (hud test asserts them). A cell's type is recognised by its mark_bits naming one static (MARK) in the core: HashLink reads a type's mark bits only to trace objects allocated under it, and nothing is allocated under a cell's type but cells, which the core traces through the descriptor. The protocol forwards every message to the object; trace marks the object (and the front); drop forgets the shadow or map entry. A holder's own constructed instance (new Hud(3)) goes in front of the cell so the object always comes back as it. A cell Haxe holds only as a pointer it never reads (callback targets, a constructed face's field) is made under a plain HABSTRACT view and re-viewed under the class on first hand-out as an object (word zero rewritten).\n\nThe views are made on first use, not at program attach: the descriptor carries the holder's language id, which the runtime only has after the world registers it (the first cut baked lang 0 in at attach time and every shadow lookup missed, +10 ns per same-object crossing).\n\nStep 1 (this issue): Wren objects in Haxe, replacing WrenRef + face. Transfer bench (200k iterations, load ~6–8), ns / B per crossing:\n\n| crossing | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| fresh object, Wren→Haxe | 120 / 160 B | 97 / 96 B |\n| returned object, Haxe→Wren | 130 / 160 B | 107 / 96 B |\n| same object, Wren→Haxe | 39 | 39 |\n\nStep 2: Haxe objects in Wren. The cell grows a Wren view: an ObjHeader at +16 (front moves to +40), Wren holding cell+16 as an instance of the installed class with no fields; +16 − PREFIX is the core start, which is how wren_lift's collector already marks through the core. Replaces the HaxeRef wrapper on every crossing and the stand-in instance; the stand-in map (Haxe object address → cell) stays until a Haxe object has a spare word. To verify: wren_lift's collector does not walk `next` on objects it did not allocate, and the escape barrier reading `flags` sees the real bytes.\n\nStep 3 (Ash, the user's call): lay out hl_type records with the seam's trailer (trace, drop, protocol, lang) so every Haxe object is a core object outright, no mirror, no wrapper.\n\nNot this issue: under ASH_GC_STRESS=1 or WLIFT_GC_STRESS=1 the interop tests crash or hang since wren_lift 249c239 (the VM/Shared split) with or without cells; tracked separately.","files":null}]}